Here is a basic example of how you could structure your `app.tsx` file to create a simple frontend for searching and displaying images from the Bing Image Search API:
```
import React, { useState } from 'react';
import axios from 'axios';

function App() {
  const [query, setQuery] = useState('');
  const [images, setImages] = useState([]);

  async function searchImages() {
    try {
      const response = await axios.get(`https://api.bing.microsoft.com/v7.0/images/search?q=${query}`);
      setImages(response.data.value);
    } catch (error) {
      console.log(error);
    }
  }

  return (
    <div>
      <input type="text" value={query} onChange={e => setQuery(e.target.value)} />
      <button onClick={searchImages}>Search</button>
      <ul>
        {images.map((image, index) => (
          <li key={index}>
            <img src={image.thumbnailUrl} alt={image.name} />
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>
    </div>
  );
}

export default App;
```
This code defines a functional component called `App`, which renders a text input field, a button, and a list of image thumbnails. When the user types in the input field and clicks the "Search" button, it makes a GET request to the Bing Image Search API with the query string as a parameter. The response data contains an array of objects representing the images that match the query. This data is then used to update the state of the `images` variable, which causes the list of image thumbnails to be re-rendered with the new data.
